<!--
  Stage-validations change (2026-08-04) — full component list. This manifest
  now carries TWO things that must deploy together: the stage-validations
  change itself, AND the Opportunity deal-actions feature as a hard
  PREREQUISITE. They cannot be split into separate deploys. CLI flags
  (dashes omitted here because XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen):
  "sf project deploy start", flag manifest=manifest/stage-validations/package.xml,
  flag post-destructive-changes=manifest/stage-validations/destructiveChangesPost.xml,
  flag test-level=RunLocalTests, flag wait=30.

  NOT YET AUTHORISED FOR DEPLOY — see the pre-deploy verification steps in
  agent-output/design-requirements-stage-validations.md §8/§10 (F2 approval-
  rejection check, F6 Lead Settings check, Lead backlog count).

  WHY post-destructive-changes FORCES A SINGLE, COMPLETE MANIFEST: sf project
  deploy start rejects flag post-destructive-changes unless flag manifest is
  also supplied (CLI error: "All of the following must be provided when
  using post-destructive-changes: manifest"), which means a flag
  source-dir-scoped deploy cannot carry the NDA rename at all. Every
  component in this change, declarative and programmatic, therefore has to
  travel through THIS ONE manifest file in a single deploy - there is no way
  to split it into a manifest deploy for the rename plus a separate
  source-dir deploy for anything else. Every member listed below was
  confirmed present on disk (.cls plus .cls-meta.xml, the LWC bundle's
  .js-meta.xml, or the QuickAction's .quickAction-meta.xml) before being
  added - a package.xml member with no matching file on disk fails deploy
  with "named in package.xml, but was not found in zipped directory".

  WHY THE DEAL-ACTIONS FEATURE IS A HARD PREREQUISITE, NOT OPTIONAL SCOPE:
  found by a dry run against the target org (deploy id 0Afiw000000EYZhCAO),
  5 component errors. The repo carries the Opportunity deal-actions feature
  (OpportunityActionPermissionService/Controller, six deal quick actions'
  LWC bundles, nine QuickActions) but the org never received it -
  StageAdvanceControllerTest :: Variable does not exist:
  OpportunityActionPermissionService (x4), and Opportunity_Record_Page ::
  couldn't validate Quick Action Opportunity.Move_to_About_to_Close.
  Dropping either half does not help: StageAdvanceControllerTest is one of
  the repaired test classes already in this manifest and references
  OpportunityActionPermissionService directly, and the Close_Deal visibility
  fix lives in the FlexiPage already in this manifest, which references
  quick actions the org does not have. The org's OLDER StageAdvanceControllerTest
  (the one it would fall back to if this class were dropped) has unrepaired
  fixtures and would start failing RunLocalTests the moment the validation
  rules go active. So the deal-actions feature is not new scope being
  smuggled in here - it is a pre-existing, already-committed gap between
  repo and org that this deploy is now forced to close as a side effect of
  the post-destructive-changes constraint above.

  UnderwritingGateTest is deliberately NOT listed - it was not modified by
  this change and needs no redeploy; it still runs under RunLocalTests.

  RED FLAG, MANDATORY POST-DEPLOY STEP FOR DPEG_Acquisitions AND
  DPEG_Apex_Access: deploying a permission set in this org has been observed
  to SILENTLY DROP FieldPermissions rows that the file still declares. This
  caused a live incident on 2026-08-01 - Broker_Protection_Access lost
  Task.Inbound_Message_Id__c, Task.Thread_Key__c and Task.WhoId, which made
  those fields invisible org-wide and broke the live Broker Protection
  pipeline; it took 60 test failures to notice before the cause was found.
  DO NOT assume a Succeeded deploy of DPEG_Acquisitions or DPEG_Apex_Access
  actually persisted every FieldPermissions row the file declares. The
  mandatory post-deploy verification is to query FieldPermissions for the
  FULL object set each of these two permission sets touches - not just the
  fields this change intended to alter - and diff the result against the
  file. Only after that diff comes back clean should either permission set
  be considered actually deployed.

  SECOND DEPENDENCY CASCADE, CLOSED VIA A FULL TRANSITIVE SWEEP (2026-08-04,
  dry run 0Afiw000000EXPHCA4, 8/46 component errors, all rooted in ONE
  missing class): OpportunityActionPermissionService references UserSelector,
  which was never deployed to the org even though PermissionSetAssignmentSelector
  (also referenced by it) already is. Fix: UserSelector and UserSelectorTest
  added below. To stop discovering this one deploy at a time, every ApexClass
  member below was then swept transitively - the org's full ApexClass list
  (270 classes) was diffed against every class name in the repo (281 classes);
  the diff closes at exactly 11 repo-only classes, all 11 of which are listed
  in the ApexClass block below, so no class referenced by anything in this
  manifest can be missing from both the org and this file. The six deal-actions
  LWC bundles were swept the same way for their flag salesforce/apex and flag
  salesforce/schema imports (StageAdvanceController and OpportunityApprovalController
  resolve against the org; OpportunityActionPermissionController resolves
  against this manifest; zero schema/field imports found). All nine QuickActions
  were confirmed to resolve to one of the five action-facing bundles below
  (dealActionGuard is a shared JS-only import, not a QuickAction target itself).
  Closure is complete - nothing referenced by any member of this manifest is
  missing from both the repo and the org.

  THIRD GAP, SECURITY-RELEVANT - THE REAL LESSON OF THIS MANIFEST'S HISTORY
  (2026-08-04, full RunLocalTests validation 0Afiw000000EYbKCAW). The org's
  copy of StageAdvanceController was STALE - zero occurrences of
  assertDealActionAccess, so advance()/advanceTo() called
  StageAdvanceService directly with NO permission gate. The deploy carried
  the NEW StageAdvanceControllerTest and the NEW OpportunityActionPermissionService,
  but not the OLD controller those were written against, so the test
  compiled clean (it only references the service, which WAS present), the
  gate never actually ran, the stage write succeeded, OpportunityReviewTrigger
  fired on it, and the failure surfaced three classes downstream as a
  QueryException from DevelopmentFeasibilityReviewSelector - nowhere near the
  real defect. Had this deployed as-is, the deal-action buttons would have
  gone LIVE WITH NO SERVER-SIDE AUTHORIZATION - client-side check only,
  trivially bypassed by calling the flag AuraEnabled method directly. Same
  incident shape as July 2026, when a quick-actions deploy shipped an
  outdated LeadConvertActionController and the convert assertion was not
  live for over a month. StageAdvanceController, StageAdvanceService,
  OpportunityApprovalController and OpportunityApprovalControllerTest are
  added below to close it - all four report changed:true, created:false,
  i.e. present in the org but stale.

  WHY THIS TOOK THREE DRY RUNS TO SURFACE, AND THE RULE FOR NEXT TIME: this
  manifest was built from the GIT delta - what this change's commits touch -
  rather than the ORG delta - what actually differs between the org and the
  repo. Those are not the same list whenever a feature has been sitting
  committed-but-never-fully-deployed, which is exactly the deal-actions
  feature's history here. RULE: for any manifest touching a feature that has
  never fully deployed, build it by diffing the org against the repo FIRST -
  flag data query "SELECT Name FROM ApexClass" versus the .cls files on disk
  (see the transitive-sweep note above for the exact method) - and only then
  narrow the result by intent. A component reported changed:true, created:false
  in a deploy result means the ORG HAS AN OLDER VERSION - that is exactly the
  silent-skew shape that hides a missing security gate behind a compiling
  test and an unrelated-looking downstream error.

  DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED, EVEN THOUGH ALSO ORG-VS-REPO DIFFERENT: EmailMessageSelector,
  EmailMessageSelectorTest, EmailThreadAnchorService, EmailThreadAnchorServiceTest,
  EmailThreadGuardService, EmailThreadGuardServiceTest, EmailThreadAdopterServiceTest.
  These are the EAC guard-5 change - committed, but DELIBERATELY held pending
  a durability experiment completing 2026-08-05. Being stale in the org right
  now is their CORRECT, intended state. Do not "helpfully" add them to this
  manifest during a future org-delta sweep just because they show up as
  different - check whether a hold is in effect before assuming every delta
  is a gap to close.

  FOURTH GAP, SAME FAILURE MODE AS THE THIRD - CAUGHT US TWICE IN ONE CHANGE
  (2026-08-04, dry run 0Afiw000000EaRpCAK: 51/51 components, 0 component
  errors, 166/167 tests, one failure). LeadConvertServiceTest.blankLeadFieldsLeaveTargetsNull
  threw the Property_And_Email_Required_To_Convert validation-rule message
  from convertOne at line 89 - but on disk convertOne is at line 103 and
  blankLeadFieldsLeaveTargetsNull at line 599. The org ran an OLDER compiled
  copy: the developer's GATE_ADDRESS fixture fix was never shipped, because
  LeadConvertServiceTest was not in this manifest. Confirmed as the only gap
  by diffing the porcelain-format flag status of force-app/main/default/classes
  (14 classes this change touches) against the manifest's members list -
  exactly one name came back uncovered. LeadConvertServiceTest is added below.

  REFINEMENT TO THE ORG-DELTA RULE ABOVE, FORCED BY THIS GAP: a name-level
  diff of repo ApexClass names against org ApexClass names - the method used
  for the transitive sweep above - only catches classes MISSING from the org.
  It is structurally blind to a class that EXISTS in the org but is STALE,
  which is exactly what LeadConvertServiceTest was, and exactly what
  StageAdvanceController was for the third gap above. Worse, this specific
  file was edited AFTER the transitive sweep ran, so even a perfect name diff
  taken at that moment would not have covered it. A correct manifest for a
  change like this needs the UNION of two different checks, and neither one
  alone is sufficient:
    (a) classes missing from the org entirely - caught by the name-diff
        method described above (misses stale-but-present classes, which is
        the whole failure mode of gaps three and four);
    (b) every class this change actually edits or adds - caught by a
        porcelain-format flag status against the classes directory (misses
        classes this change does NOT touch but that a touched class newly
        depends on, which is the whole failure mode of gap two, UserSelector).
  Both halves of this change were bitten by exactly one of these two gaps in
  isolation - proof that relying on either check alone is not enough. Check
  (b) must also be RE-RUN after any late edit to a file already in the
  manifest, since the manifest is a point-in-time snapshot and fixture
  repairs (like the GATE_ADDRESS fix here) routinely land after it is first
  written.
-->
